GPS: billions of users, every day, the most successful space system ever built.

But GPS doesn't reach the Moon.

Galileo doesn't reach the Moon.

ESA's Moonlight programme is building "Galileo for the Moon" — communications, navigation, and timing for future lunar missions.

ESA signed the Moonlight LCNS contract in October 2024 with a consortium led by Telespazio. It's not a concept — it's in procurement.

We are already designing infrastructure beyond Earth.

Slide 7 — The Internet Has the Same Problem

The Internet assumes:

Fast responses (TCP handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)
Continuous connections
Stable routes

Space provides:

Long delays
Intermittent links
Orbital motion

Slide 8 — Why TCP Breaks in Space

Show a TCP handshake over a lunar link:

SYN → (1.3 seconds) → arrives at Moon
SYN-ACK → (1.3 seconds) → arrives back at Earth
ACK → (1.3 seconds) → arrives at Moon

3.9 seconds just to say hello. Before any data moves.

Now try Mars: 3–22 minutes each way.

A TCP handshake to Mars could take over an hour.

NASA's own assessment (2023): "DTN automatically ensures the delivery of information using store-and-forward, which enables internet-like concepts in space."

Slide 9 — Distances That Break Your Brain

Destination         One-way Delay
London–New York     ~30 ms
GEO Satellite       ~120 ms
Moon                ~1.3 s
Mars                3–22 min
Jupiter             35–50 min

What does a Zoom call to Mars look like?

You say hello.
You wait 22 minutes.
They say hello back.
You wait another 22 minutes to hear it.

The speed of light is not a software bug you can patch.

So the architecture is simple:

On the Moon surface? Normal internet — just like a LAN.
Between Earth and Moon? The bundle postal service.

[Speaker note: For network engineers — the SSI report specifies IPv6 locally on celestial bodies, BP/LTP for long-haul between worlds, and no IPv4 in space, ever. You can drop this detail verbally if the audience seems technical enough.]

Slide 10 — "But What About Starlink?"

