Slide 12 — A Solar System Postal Service

Image:

docs/diagrams/store-and-forward.svg (dark-themed, shows full sequence: upload → store in NVM → no contact → forward → ACK)

Introduce:

Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN)

Simple explanation:

Store
Carry
Forward
Deliver

No continuous connection required.

A bundle (like a parcel) is handed off node to node until it reaches its destination. If the next hop isn't available, the node holds onto it.

When a bundle is "in flight" for 22 minutes to Mars — the sending node is responsible for it until the next node confirms receipt.

Slide 13 — Routing by Time

Image:

docs/diagrams/contact-graph.svg (dark-themed, shows scheduled pass windows with CGR-computed route)

Visual:

Earth visible to relay 12:00–12:10
Relay visible to Moon 12:30–12:40

In space, you often know future connectivity in advance.

You know the satellite will be visible at 14:32 tomorrow.

So you schedule the transmission now.

This is Contact Graph Routing — the network equivalent of a train timetable.

For anyone who's only worked with IP routing, this is genuinely alien.

The IETF now has a dedicated Time-Variant Routing (TVR) Working Group standardising this concept for broader networking — it's not just a space thing anymore.

And here's a detail that'll amuse you: the SSI report discusses the need for UTC(Moon) — a separate lunar time standard. Relativistic effects at cislunar distances mean clocks on the Moon drift relative to Earth. Your contact plan literally needs a different clock.

Slide 14 — It's Already Running in Space

Images:

ISS photograph (NASA public domain — "HDTN is running here")
KPLO spacecraft render (KARI press kit — "BPv7 tested here, at the Moon")
Side-by-side layout with captions

This is not theoretical:

The International Space Station runs HDTN right now — NASA Glenn's DTN engine, acting as a gateway between payloads and ground. Won an R&D 100 Award in 2025.

The Korea Pathfinder Lunar Orbiter (KPLO) tested Bundle Protocol at the Moon in 2024.

ION-DTN (JPL) has flight heritage on multiple deep-space missions.

ESA is standardising it through CCSDS.

These protocols are operational. In space. Today.

Slide 15 — The Solar System Internet

Image:

docs/images/nasa-dtn/nasa-dtn-networking-abstract.jpg (NASA DTN concept graphic)
Or: a simplified diagram of the "network of networks" vision (Earth → GEO relay → Moon → Mars)

In 2023, the Internet Society's Interplanetary Chapter (IPNSIG) published a governance framework for the Solar System Internet — co-authored by Vint Cerf.

Their recommendation:

"DTN and the BP suite should be the basis for all communications that might have to traverse paths that the IP protocol suite cannot support."

That's Vint Cerf, NASA, ESA, CCSDS, and the IETF converging on one answer.

The vision — and this is a direct quote from the report:

"In 1983, three disparate networks (Arpanet, SATNET, PRNET) joined together using TCP/IP, giving birth to the operational Internet. Establishing a shared and interoperable network in space will follow a similar trajectory."

The evolution:

Today: Space agency point-to-point links
Transitional: Commercial and amateur networks emerge alongside agency backbones
Future: Multiple interconnected networks, peering like ISPs on Earth

The report explicitly calls for multistakeholder governance:

"Policy decisions should be made collaboratively amongst all stakeholders."

They want non-agency stakeholders involved from the earliest stages.

Amateur radio operators are a stakeholder.

RADIANT is how we earn our seat at that table.

