Slide 21 — Why This Matters

This isn't really about amateur radio.

It's about:

Infrastructure
Exploration
Open systems
The future of networking

All protocols are public. All software is open source. Anyone can run a node.

The regulations that govern amateur radio mean everything we build is transparent and auditable by design.

NASA's LunaNet Interoperability Specification mandates the same protocols we implement. RADIANT isn't building something incompatible — it's building something that could interoperate with NASA's lunar infrastructure.

A governance insight: every DTN node needs a unique address. The SSI report worries about who allocates these fairly. Turns out amateur radio already has one — your callsign. It's globally unique, government-issued, and the allocation system has worked since 1927. No blockchain required.

The IPNSIG report's key principles for the Solar System Internet:

Collaboration
Transparency
Interoperability
Fair resource allocation
Security built in from the start

RADIANT already embodies all of these.

