Speaker Notes

Pacing: 24 slides for 40 minutes = ~1.7 minutes per slide. Comfortable pace with room for pauses and audience reactions.

Suggested time budget:

| Section | Slides | Time |
|---------|--------|------|
| Hook + context | 1–6 | 8 min |
| The problem | 7–11 | 8 min |
| DTN concepts | 12–13 | 5 min |
| Professional context | 14–15 | 5 min |
| Amateur + RADIANT | 16–18 | 5 min |
| Demo | 19 | 3 min |
| Partners + why | 20–21 | 3 min |
| Close + Q&A prep | 22–24 | 3 min |

~2 minutes buffer for pauses and audience reactions.

Key principles for EMF:

- The audience is makers, hackers, artists, crafters, scientists, and engineers. Not all of them are network engineers. Keep it accessible.
- The audience leaves talking about "building infrastructure beyond Earth", not about protocol stacks.
- Use analogies they know: UUCP, postal service, train timetables.
- Pre-empt the Starlink question — it shows you've thought about it.
- The TCP handshake example makes the problem visceral for anyone who's written networked code. For non-coders, the "3.9 seconds just to say hello" framing still works.
- The "speed of light is not a firmware update" line gets a laugh and makes the point.
- The IPNSIG/SSI reference adds institutional weight — this isn't just amateur radio people dreaming.
- The HDTN R&D 100 Award and ISS deployment prove this is operational, not academic.
- The KPLO reference proves DTN is at the Moon today — not "someday".
- The 1983 Arpanet/SATNET/PRNET quote is the money line — it connects everyone's knowledge of Internet history to this vision.
- The IETF TVR Working Group reference is the "wait, the IETF is standardising this?" moment for network engineers.
- For Rust developers: Hardy's `no_std` core and availability on crates.io gives them an immediate engagement path.
- For embedded/microcontroller people: µD3TN targets their world directly. The STM32U585 reference on slide 18 makes it tangible ("same chip as your badge hack").
- The "No blockchain required" line on slide 21 gets a laugh at EMF and makes the governance point.
- The LunaNet reference on slide 21 makes the point that RADIANT could interoperate with NASA infrastructure — powerful for credibility.
- The demo (slide 19) is the centrepiece for this audience. Give it 2-3 minutes. Seeing it work beats explaining it.
- Explicitly invite people to "clone and run" — EMF attendees will go back to their tent and try it.
- End on the emotional line (slide 22), keep the logistics (slide 23) and references (slide 24) as a backdrop for Q&A.
- Cross-reference the AMSAT Village session to funnel interested technical people.
- QR code on slide 23 — people will photograph it.
- Leave slide 24 (Further Reading) visible during Q&A so people can photograph the URLs.
- If families are in the audience: the "Imagine it's 2055, you live on the Moon" opening works for all ages. The postal analogy works for kids too.

Audience-specific hooks:

- Software developers → demo, Hardy crate, open-source contribution path
- Hardware engineers → STM32U585, CubeSat EM, embedded µD3TN
- Network engineers → IPv6/BP split, IETF TVR WG, CGR as "train timetable routing"
- Space enthusiasts → KPLO, LunaNet, cislunar Phase 4 ambition
- Amateur radio operators → AMSAT Village, callsign-EIDs, regulatory compliance
- Curious non-specialists → postal service analogy, 2055 opener, Zoom-to-Mars joke
- Artists/makers → "anyone can run a node", open source, the infrastructure-as-art framing

